course: dictionary
lesson: 845
title: Besiege: surrounded and under pressure
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To besiege something means to surround it so that nobody can get in or out. In history, an army would besiege a castle or walled town, and the people inside could run short of food. «Attackers besieged castles for weeks or months with catapults and trebuchets, or tried to starve the defenders out.» The clue is the words for and out: the attackers were waiting for weeks, and they wanted to starve the people inside.
explain: Besiege has a second meaning, and you can use the same check to understand it. «Fans besieged the singer» means the fans crowded all around her, so she could not move freely. A crowd can besiege a person just as an army can besiege a city, though a crowd does not usually mean any harm.
explain: The third meaning is about questions and requests. If someone is besieged by reporters, they are pestered with so many questions that they cannot get on with their day. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check: «The mayor was besieged by reporters» still makes sense if you think of a crowd pressing in with questions.
fact: Besiege means to surround a place or a person so that they are trapped or cannot escape.
fact: «Besieged» is the past tense: «The Mongols besieged walled cities» means they surrounded them in the past.
fact: In history, sieges could last for weeks, months or even years, and hunger inside the walls was often a serious problem.
fact: «Cannons from the 1400s made castle walls far less useful.» This is why the word siege is linked to castles in history lessons.
fact: Homer's poems tell of the Greeks besieging Troy for ten years, but the wooden horse is a story from legend, not proven history.
exercise: «Attackers besieged castles for weeks or months.» What does besieged mean here? | Painted in bright colours | *Surrounded so that nobody could get in or out | Sold to a new owner | Left empty for a holiday
exercise: «Fans besieged the singer after the concert.» What does besieged mean here? | Sang along to | Ran away from | *Crowded around | Waited quietly for
exercise: «The mayor was besieged by reporters.» What does this tell you? | She had won a prize for her garden | Nobody came to see her at all | *She was pestered by a crowd of people with many questions | She was feeling sleepy after lunch
test: «The Mongols used Chinese and Persian engineers to besiege walled cities.» What does besiege mean in this sentence? | Dance around a fire | *Surround so that nobody can get in or out | Build a new road | Sing a song
test: Homer's poems tell of «the Greeks besieging Troy for ten years.» Is this a proven history fact or a story from legend? | *The poems are a story from legend, and the wooden horse is legend too | A made-up joke with no meaning | Proven history, because it has a date | Proven history, because the Greeks wrote it themselves
exam: Which sentence uses besiege to mean surround with questions? | The castle stood on a hill above the sea | *The reporters besieged the minister with questions all afternoon | The army besieged the city wall for ten years | The children besieged the teacher with questions after the lesson
